{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices, especially beyond ~day 8–10. Apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Over-optimistic rebound/mean-reversion: after a downtrend, the model projects a bounce back into the low/mid-70s that does not materialize. Damp mean-reversion strength (pull forecasts toward the most recent price/trend rather than the longer-run average).",
    "Trend lag: the model reacts too slowly to sustained declines; realized prices keep falling while forecasts flatten/rise. Add a trend-following adjustment: if the last 10–20 days slope is negative, subtract an extra amount proportional to that slope across the forecast path.",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: errors grow with lead time; long-horizon forecasts overshoot most. Use a tapering correction where the downward adjustment ramps up with forecast day (e.g., linear or piecewise: minimal days 1–5, moderate days 6–15, strongest days 16–30).",
    "Peak/plateau overstatement: when the model forms a local peak/plateau (often days ~10–18), realized outcomes are materially lower. Cap local maxima relative to the starting level (e.g., limit any rise above the day-0 price unless recent momentum is positive)."
  ]
}
